1,2 $ Context almost instantly discolouring greyish to blackish when bruised
1,9 $ Context not discolouring instantly greyish blackish when bruised, sometimes discolouring greyish after several hours
2,0 $ Hymenium yellowish $ L. crocipodium (13)
2,3 $ Hymenium whitish, greyish or brownish
3,4 $ Margin of the pileus, especially in young fruit-bodies, overhanging with disrupted flaps, colour of pileus usually reddish brown or orange, sometimes pinkish or whitish
3,8 $ Margin of the pileus entire, only slightly projecting; colour of pileus brownish
4,5 $ Stipe ornamentation whitish, brownish to reddish brown, in older specimens sometimes almost blackish; content of caulocystidia reddish brown; associated with broadleaved trees
4,6 $ Stipe ornamentation brown or black, content of pigmented caulocystidia greyish, associated with Pinaceae or Betula
5,0 $ Stipe in young fruit-bodies with reddish brown squamules, which become darker with age; pileus reddish brown, dark (reddish) brown in exsiccates $ L. aurantiacum (1)
5,0 $ Stipe in young fruit-bodies with whitish squamules, which become darker only in very mature stage; pileus orange, light brown in exsiccates $ L. albostipitatum (2)
6,0 $ Pileus colour usually orange or brownish orange, largest caulocystidia generally utriform, fusiform, sometimes lageniform, associated with Betula $ L. versipelle (4)
6,7 $ Pileus brownish to reddish brown, largest caulocystidia generally clavate, associated with Pinaceae
7,0 $ Pileus reddish brown, associated with Pinus or Picea $ L. vulpinum (3)
7,0 $ Pileus dull brownish, associated with Picea $ L. piceinum (see discussion L. vulpinum)
8,0 $ Surface of pileus wrinkled in young fruit-bodies; pileipellis a palisade trichoderm of broadly clavate elements, terminal elemets often globose; associated with Corylus or Carpinus $ L. pseudoscabrum (4)
8,0 $ Surface of pileus never wrinkled; pileipellis a cutis with ellipsoid or filiform terminal elements; associated with Populus $ L. duriusculum (5)
9,10 $ Cylindrocysts in pileipellis abundant; stipe base usually with clear greenish bluish stains
9,11 $ Cylindrocysts in pileipellis scarce; stipe base with or without bluish stains
10,0 $ Stipe squamules coarse and usually concolorous to stipe surface, forming a fibrillose dense layer; pileus evenly coloured; pileipellis elements usually easily detachable, often broad (8-15 yim); mean Q-value spores > 3.0 $ L. cyaneobasileucum (11)
10,0 $ Stipe squamules fine, usually blackish, greyish and contrasting to the stipe surface; pileus often dark-coloured with lighter regions or light-coloured with darker regions; pileipellis consisting of an intricate cutis of filamentous hyphae, often with chains of cylindrocysts in the suprapellis; mean Q-value spores < 2.8 $ L. variicolor (10)
11,0 $ Stipe surface dark (greyish, blackish) almost concolorous to stipe squamules $ L. melaneum (7)
11,12 $ Stipe surface light, brownish, strongly contrasting to squamules when squamules are darkly coloured, otherwise squamules light and not contrasting to the surface
12,0 $ Mean Q-value spores z3.0, usually large clavate caulocystidia present in caulohymenium, bluish discolorations absent in stipe base $ L. scabrum (6)
12,13 $ Mean Q-value spores < 3.0, no large clavate caulocystidia present in caulohymenium, bluish stains absent or present in stipe base
13,0 $ Caulocystidia often clavate, sometimes lageniform; bluish discoloration in stipe base absent $ L. rotundifoliae (8)
13,14 $ Caulocystidia narrowly lageniform with a flexuose neck or utriform, rarely clavate; bluish discoloration in stipe base present or absent
14,0 $ Stipe ornamentation composed of coarse warty or flocculose squamules; caulocystidia often narrowly lageniform with a flexuose neck; pileipellis elements with a vacuolar pigment or granualar incrustation; pileus usually whitish, light brownish, sometimes with bluish or greenish tinges $ L. holopus (12)
14,0 $ Stipe ornamentation composed of fine greyish or blackish squamules; caulocystidia often utriform, rarely lageniform with a flexuose neck; pileipellis elements often incrusted with dark plaques of pigment; pileus greyish or brownish $ L. schistophilum (9)
